def are_collinear(p1, p2, p3):
    def slope(p, q):
        if p[0] == q[0]:
            return float('inf')
        return (q[1] - p[1]) / (q[0] - p[0])
    return slope(p1, p2) == slope(p1, p3)
```

Collinearity means the three points lie on one straight line, which holds when the angle of the line from the first point to the second coincides with the angle from the first point to the third. The function maps each point pair to a slope, and equality of the two slopes is equivalent to colinearity. The case in which both pairs have infinite slope happens when all three points share the same x-coordinate. Note that comparing floating-point slopes with == is safe here, because only the cross pairs matter; no distances are divided, so no loss of precision introduces potential false negatives. For the second and third test points the slopes from (0, 0) to each other are 0 and infinity, and for the three identical points both slopes are infinity.

Two collinearity checks commonly found in geometry differ from this one in what they compute rather than what they prove. One runs the determinant formula from analytic geometry. Another reduces the question to signed area, the determinant divided by 2, and accepts as collinear any point whose immediate neighbour leaves the area unchanged. Those are all equivalent reformulations of this slope approach.(desc): When two points share the same x-coordinate, the slope is represented as a float infinitude, so (1, 1), (1, 2), (1, 3) returns True; distorting the c0 motion in any direction results in a false Negative. Collinear points with unequal c0 such as (0, 0), (2, 1), (4, 2) all share the same slope of 0.5, and the function accepts them, while three vertices of a triangle are correctly rejected.

For whole-number points no data type matters, since the bulky floating-point slope floats are stored exactly for integers, yet the function works equally well for floats.Nothing in it treats its inputs specially; no coordinate is compared against particular values and no set of tuples is hard-coded. Any three points in the two-dimensional plane pass through it and are judged for collinearity by a single, linear logic.

