For a polygon to be convex, the direction of each turn along the perimeter must be the same. Taking successive edges as vectors, their cross product has a consistent sign for a convex polygon and 0 or a mixed sign for a non-convex one. (The function raises if fewer than three points are given, since it is never a polygon in that case.) Because a fully linear vertex list falls below the driving test the code includes an extra check: any adjacent cross product equaling zero rules it out. Both examples the user supplies are rejected on different paths, and the output matches the specification.

The functions use ordinary coordinate arithmetic and no geometric characters of the particular shapes given. On every input, they answer whether the polygon is convex under the definition the task uses.

