The Chebyshev distance between two points (x1, y1) and (x2, y2) is the maximum of the absolute differences of their coordinates, max(|x1 - x2|, |y1 - y2|). The function returns that value by taking the absolute difference along each axis and choosing the larger. None of the coordinates are shifted before the difference is taken, since the distance measures only displacement from one point to the other. The implementation works for any pair of tuples of floats, and both empty asserts evaluate to 0.

For integers, it comes out the same: (2, 2) to (5, 3) has a difference of 3 along the x-axis and 1 along the y-axis, so it yields 3, as the third assert requires. The abs calls make the result non-negative even when one point lies west or south of the other.

